@page "/getting-started"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Getting Started";
}

<h1>Getting Started</h1>

<p>
    This guide takes you from nothing to a running game: installing TypeDitor, setting up the
    development prerequisites and building a first small project.
</p>

<h2>Prerequisites</h2>
<p>
    TypeDitor builds your game from C# source while you work, and both the installer and the editor use the
    <code>typer</code> tooling under the hood. Make sure the following are installed and available on your
    <code>PATH</code>:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download" rel="noopener">.NET SDK 10.0</a> or later &mdash; compiles TypeDitor, the modules and your game.</li>
    <li><a href="https://git-scm.com/" rel="noopener">Git</a> &mdash; used to fetch TypeDitor and module versions from GitHub.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/" rel="noopener">Python 3</a> &mdash; runs <code>typer</code>, which generates project files and resolves module dependencies.</li>
    <li>Optional but recommended: a C# IDE such as Visual Studio, Rider or VS Code for writing your game code.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Install TypeDitor</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li>
        <strong>Download TypeDater</strong> for your platform from the <a asp-page="/Download">download page</a>
        and extract it into an empty folder where you want TypeDitor to live, for example
        <code>C:\Tools\TypeDitor</code> or <code>~/typeditor</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Run TypeDater</strong>. When there is no TypeDitor next to it, it switches to install mode:
        it fetches the latest TypeDitor release, generates the project files with typer and builds the editor.
        This can take a few minutes the first time.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Start TypeDitor</strong> from the same folder. Later on, running TypeDater again (or letting
        TypeDitor check for updates) updates the installation in place.
    </li>
</ol>

<h2>Your first project</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li>
        <strong>Create a new project</strong> in TypeDitor. A TypeO project is described by a
        <code>typeo.json</code> file that lists its name, version and module dependencies.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Add modules.</strong> For a 2D desktop game you typically want
        <code>TypeOCore</code> (always required), <code>TypeOBasic2d</code>, <code>TypeODesktop</code> and
        <code>TypeOTK</code>. TypeDitor downloads each module version, resolves their dependencies and builds them.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Explore the generated code.</strong> TypeDitor generates the program entry point for you.
        For a game called <code>MyGame</code> it looks like this:
<pre><code>TypeO.Create&lt;MyGame&gt;("MyGame")
    .LoadModule&lt;Basic2dModule&gt;()
    .LoadModule&lt;DesktopModule&gt;()
    .LoadModule&lt;TKModule&gt;()
    .Start();</code></pre>
        Your game class derives from <code>Game</code> and is where you set up the window, input and your
        first scene:
<pre><code>partial class MyGame : Game
{
    public WindowService WindowService { get; set; }
    public KeyboardInputService KeyboardInputService { get; set; }

    protected void InternalInitialize()
    {
        KeyboardInputService.SetKeyAlias("Quit", KeyboardKey.Escape);
        MainWindow = WindowService.CreateWindow("MyGame", new Vec2i(100, 100), new Vec2i(1280, 720));
    }

    protected void InternalCleanup()
    {
    }
}</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Add components.</strong> Create scenes, entities and drawables as components in TypeDitor.
        The editor generates the plumbing into <code>.typed.cs</code> files and leaves matching partial
        classes for your own logic. Custom properties you add in the editor become C# properties on
        your components.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Run it.</strong> Press play in TypeDitor to build and run your game inside the editor
        viewport, complete with selection, move and resize tools &mdash; or run the generated solution
        straight from your IDE.
    </li>
</ol>

